    "body": "Let's check what browsers do today. Each case is a small HTML page in its own iframe, with an SVG script element in an inline SVG. The script is a data: URL that posts a message to the page, so I can tell whether it ran. The examples below are shortened: the real script also sends `document.readyState` and whether the last paragraph of the page exists yet, which is what cases 8 and 9 are judged on.\n\nCases 4 to 6 add a Content Security Policy first, set with a meta element. `script-src data:` blocks inline scripts and allows the data: URL.\n\n```html\n<!-- 1 -->\n<svg><script href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 2 -->\n<svg><script xlink:href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 3: two different scripts, to see which one is used -->\n<svg><script href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('href','*')\"\n             xlink:href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('xlink','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- cases 4 to 6 start with this -->\n<meta http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\" content=\"script-src data:\">\n\n<!-- 4 -->\n<svg><script href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 5 -->\n<svg><script xlink:href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 6, control: inline text -->\n<svg><script>parent.postMessage('ran','*')</script></svg>\n\n<!-- 7 -->\n<svg><script nomodule href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 8 -->\n<svg><script async href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- 9 -->\n<svg><script defer href=\"data:text/javascript,parent.postMessage('ran','*')\"/></svg>\n\n<!-- every case ends with -->\n<p id=\"after\">after</p>\n```\n\n| Case | Safari 27.0 | Chrome 158.0.0.0 | Firefox 160.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 href | runs | runs | runs |\n| 2 xlink:href | runs | runs | runs |\n| 3 both | `href` used | `href` used | `href` used |\n| **4 href, with the policy** | **runs** | **runs** | **runs** |\n| **5 xlink:href, with the policy** | **runs** | **runs** | **runs** |\n| 6 inline text, with the policy (control) | blocked | blocked | blocked |\n| 7 nomodule | runs | runs | runs |\n| 8 async | async | async | async |\n| **9 defer** | **not deferred** | **not deferred** | **deferred** |\n\nThe bold rows are the ones that decide anything.\n\nTo remind what the HTML spec says, for a script like case 4, which has an href and nothing written inside the element:\n\n- [If el has no src attribute, and source text is the empty string, then return](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#script-processing-empty). Read literally, it stops here.\n- [If el does not have a src content attribute, and the Should element's inline behavior be blocked by Content Security Policy? algorithm returns Blocked](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#script-processing-csp). Read literally, the script is checked as inline and the policy blocks it.\n- [If el has a src content attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#script-processing-src-prepare). Read literally, the fetch is skipped.\n\nNo engine does that. All three use href as the source, and a policy that blocks inline scripts does not block it. The text is what is unclear here, not the behavior.\n\nThe only difference is defer. SVG 2 does not list a defer attribute for script, and Firefox honors it anyway.\n\nFor Safari 27.0, Firefox 160.0 and Chrome 158.0.0.0, at least, all browsers engines agree on everything else in the table.",
